{
  "type": "say",
  "text": "Good question, Nadia. Here's how I want to sequence this. Within the 10-day window, we should file two things simultaneously: first, a motion for a HIPAA-qualified protective order covering the 86 spreadsheets Marcus identified, and second, a proposed source-code inspection protocol that tracks the license restrictions — secure review room, outside counsel only, no downloads, no employee access. Filing them together serves two strategic purposes: it shows the court we are engaging substantively with the discovery demand rather than stonewalling, and it frames both restrictions as legally compelled — HIPAA for the spreadsheets, contractual license obligations for the source code — rather than as tactical withholding. That framing matters because the plaintiff has signaled they will challenge any substantial restriction. We should also revise the draft response to explicitly state that ordinary document production will proceed for all non-affected materials, while the 86 spreadsheets will be produced in de-identified, redacted form pending entry of the HIPAA-qualified order, and source code will be available for inspection only. One thing I want to confirm with both of you: Marcus, do we have the resources to complete de-identification and redaction of all 86 spreadsheets within the 10-day window, or do we need to propose a staggered production schedule in our response? And Nadia, has the client's privacy office formally documented the license restriction in writing, or do we need to obtain a declaration to attach to our filing? I want to make sure we have evidentiary support for both restrictions before we serve anything."
}
```

